Introduction
	XFaceMaker provides a powerful way of defining re-usable modules
	by means of templates. Templates are a kind of model or pattern
	that can be instanciated. A template instance is similar to a	
	copy of the pattern, but when the definition of the pattern is
	changed, all its instances are changed automatically. Template
	instances inherit resources, active values and create callbacks
	from their template. Templates can also be composite object: in
	that case, the children of the template are created automatically
	when an instance is created.
	
	Templates can be defined in default mode, in which case the inherited
	attributes can be changed after instanciation, or in override mode,
	in which case inherited attributes cannot be changed.
	
	Templates can also be applied to existing widgets, thus allowing
	you to define style sheets to enforce particular resources homogeously
	in a project.
	
	Several templates can be applied to a single widget, and templates
	can `nest', i.e. a template can be defined using another template.
	
Creating or Loading a Template
	To create a template, you must first define the object which will
	be used the model of the template. The model object is created like
	any ordinary interface object. The model can be a single widget,
	or a composite widget with sub-widgets.
	
	Once the model object is defined, you must select it and call the
	command `Modules/Templates/Save As' in the main menu bar of XFaceMaker.
	This will pop up a special purpose file selector in which you must
	specify:
	
	- the name of the template (the name will be used to associate the
	template to its instances),
	
	- the template mode (default or override),
	
	- the .fm file where the template definition will be saved.
	
	When you click `OK', the selected object is saved in the specified .fm
	file, and the template definition is recorded by XFaceMaker with the
	specified name and mode. An icon is created in the toolkit area of
	the main window, in the `Templates' section: to see it, use the Toolkit
	pulldown menu and select `Templates'.
	
	You can also load a template that was defined previously. To do this,
	use the command `Modules/Templates/Load'. The template selector pops
	up and lets you choose the template name, mode and file. Note that the
	template mode is not saved in the .fm file, so you may load a template
	with a different mode than when it was saved. You may also re-load
	a template with a different mode.
	
	Note that the `File/Save Prefs' command records the templates that
	you have saved or loaded, so you don't have to load them again when
	you restart XFaceMaker. Loaded templates are also recorded in project
	files.
	
Creating a Template Instance
	To create an instance of a template:
	
	- select the `Templates' button in the Toolkit menu,
	
	- click on the icon of the template you want to instanciate,
	
	- place the object as usual in your interface; if you do not
	specify a size for the instance using the rubber-band, the
	instance takes the size of the model object of the template.
	
	The instance looks like a copy of the template model. When you
	select the instance to edit its resources, you will see in the
	resource list that attributes inherited from the template are
	marked with the `template' icon or the [T] letter (if the
	template was in default mode), or are listed as restricted
	resources (if the template was in override mode).
	
	You can modify the resources inherited by the instance of
	a default mode template. In that case, the resource becomes
	marked as `set' (letter [S]). If you modify the resource again
	and enter the original template value, the resource state goes
	back to `template'.
	
	In the textual resource editor (i.e. the text field displayed
	by the `Edit' button of the resource window), inherited resources
	are marked with a `*' (default) or `**' (override) prefix.
	
	Note: When you load an interface containing instances of a
	template that is not already loaded, the template is
	automatically loaded by XFaceMaker.
	
Applying and Un-Applying Templates
	A template can be applied to an existing object. You can apply
	templates in three ways:
	
	1. Call one of the commands `Edit/Apply Template/...'.
	These commands pop up a prompt box where you must choose the name
	of the template you want to apply. Choose a template and click
	`OK'. The template will be applied, depending on the command used:
	
	- to the selected object (`To Selected Object' command),
	
	- to the selected object and all its sub-widgets (`To Whole Subtree'
	command),
	
	- to the selected object and all its sub-widgets which are of
	the same class as the template model (`To Widgets Of Same Class'
	command).
	
	2. Select `Templates' in the Toolkit menu and click the right mouse
	button on icon of the template you want to apply. A menu appears, with
	the same commands as in the main menu. Here, you do not need to enter
	the template name.
	
	3. Edit the `Templates' text field in the resource window. You can
	add one or several templates separated by commas. Then click on the
	Rebuild button.
	
	When applying a template, the resources and other attributes of the
	template are applied to
	the object, and the object is rebuilt. Applying a single template
	to an object of the same class as the template model is equivalent
	to creating an instance of the template as explained before.
	
	The Apply commands, though, make it possible to do more than a simple
	instanciation:
	
	- You can apply several templates to the same object (this is known
	in object-oriented languages as multiple inheritance). In that case,
	if several templates define the same attributes with different values,
	the last template applied takes precedence. The list of templates of
	an object are displayed in the `Templates' text field in the resource
	window.
	
	- You can un-apply templates using the `Un-apply' menus. When you
	un-apply a template, the attributes inherited from the template
	(and that have not been modified in the instance) are removed.
	
Inherited Attributes
	An instance of a template inherits the following attributes from
	its template:
	
	- all the resources set in the template model, except the x, y
	resources if the model had the `autopos' flag set, and except
	the width, height resources if the model had the `autosize' flag
	set (see also `Composite Templates);
	
	- all the active values of the template model;
	
	- the create callback of the template model;
	
	- the `popup' and `gadget' flags and, for sub-templates, the
	`mapped', `autosize' and `autopos' flags (see also `Composite
	Templates).
	
	The template flag values (popup, etc.) always override the flags of
	the instance (event of the template is in default mode): they
	cannot be modified, and are not restored if the template is
	un-applied.
	
Editing a Template
	A template can be edited even after it is instancianted. To
	edit a template, call the command `Modules/Templates/Edit',
	which will prompt you for a template name. The template model
	is loaded and displayed in a toplevel shell of its own. If
	the template is already in edition, XFaceMaker displays a
	warning box.
	
	Once you are done editing the template, you must select the
	toplevel widget of the model and call the command
	`Modules/Templates/Save'. The template will be re-defined with
	the modified model object. If instances of the template exist
	in the interface you are editing, they will be rebuilt to
	take the new definition into account.
	
Composite Templates
	Template models can be composite objects with sub-objects. When
	you instanciate a composite template, the children of the model
	object are created automatically as children of the instance. These
	automatic children are marked with the `**' prefix in the Widget
	Tree or the Widget List.
	
	You cannot destroy a child inherited from a template. More exactly,
	you can destroy it but it will be re-created automatically the
	next time you rebuild the template instance.
	
	When a composite template is created, XFaceMaker creates automatically
	a template for all of its sub-widgets. Every child created automatically
	in the instance is an instance of the corresponding sub-template.
	
	For children of composite templates, the `mapped', `autopos' and
	`autosize' flags are copied from the template model (whereas they
	are not copied for the toplevel template).
	
Nested Templates
	Templates can nest: you can create an instance of template T1, edit
	it, then save it as template T2. This can be repeated at any depth.
	XFaceMaker checks that you do not create an infinite loop of
	templates (i.e. T1 is an instance of T2 ... which is an instance of
	T1).
	
	When a nested template is instanciated, the `deepest' templates are
	applied first.
